A||N||14||LT----1||Bouncy Front End With KONIS||||11||
Z||000000||LT----1||01-31-2000||02:01 AM||||I have a V8 engine in an 84 fiero. Shocks are new (koni's) but the front end seems a little too light...bouncy on uneven roads.<BR>I have adjusted the shocks to a stiffer position which improved the ride. It was bouncier on medium to softer settings. Any ideas on what causes the bouncing...springs?<BR>All front end parts are new, ball joints, alignment, tires, shocks, sway bar bushings, bearings, hubs, etc. What's left?||||reg||11
Z||000001||Standard||01-31-2000||03:44 AM||||I don't know how the Koni's adjust, but it sounds like you need some more rebound damping.  The V8 does shift the weight bias more towards the rear... might want to try going to a lighter spring winding?  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||01-31-2000||07:49 AM||||I have konis and it was explained to me that on firm the suspension rides on the shocks and on soft the suspension rides on the springs. There is much more to it than that but it all boils down to the same thing.||||reg||1
Z||000003||LT----1||01-31-2000||12:34 PM||||So do I adjust the shocks to (+) stiffer or<BR>(-) softer? The front end lifts up about<BR>1-2 inches. It almost makes it look like the front end is too light. I have put some weight there:<P>1. One small jack<BR>2. One v8 steel crankshaft<P>But i might need sandbags!||||reg||5
Z||000004||batboy||01-31-2000||12:40 PM||||I've noticed that the front end of my V8 Fiero feels a little light and "floaty" at high speed. It's probably more of it being tail heavy than a front suspension problem. I do have heavier front springs though, so I don't notice it being all that bouncy. If you haven't relocated the battery up front or added a rear sway bar yet, then that's what I would do first.||||reg||1
Z||000005||LT----1||01-31-2000||03:01 PM||||My battery is in the trunk. I am thinking that I need to add more weight in front so see if it bounces less. This is usually a problem with bad roads but I notice other cars on the same roads and they go right over the bumps and holes without affect the front end stability of the car that much. Given most are front engine cars...still i need to do something about this problem.||||reg||2
Z||000006||batboy||01-31-2000||03:24 PM||||The battery was in the rear trunk of my car too when I bought it, which is the worst place to put a battery in a Fiero. Moving it front will take about 25 pounds off the rear and put it up front. Should be a noticeable difference in the weight distribution. I'm also going to eventually use aluminum heads on my 350 V8 which should shave off another 40 pounds off the rear. Another thing I plan to do is to install a good front air dam to help prevent high speed front-end lift. Slap a 50 pound sandbag up front on top of the spare tire for a couple days and see if that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000007||lowCG||01-31-2000||05:34 PM||||Old Porsches used ballast in the front to help with the same problem,so don't feel too bad.I know that our cars need a pretty soft shock in the compression(down)stroke,but then need a lot of rebound(up)control.Some adjustable Konis have the adjustment for the rebound only,but the new design may be different.The old rebuildable-type Konis in my Jetta can be made to lift the front wheels off the ground after hitting a bump if I over adjust them.The stock Fiero shocks are setup with alot of rebound control,as are Monroe,uh,I forget the model,but they're blue,and are the sporty model.<BR>A progressive rate spring will help also.||||reg||1
Z||000008||LT----1||01-31-2000||05:49 PM||||Yeah. You guys made good observations. I have the aluminum head, ZZ1 engine which is lighter than the typical sb chevy. So that helps. I am not sure about moving the battery to the front but that's not a bad idea except I worry about such long (+) cables cause you lose power in the circuitry.<BR>I also have an MSD 6AL ignition - wow that made a big difference - hooked up in the trunk. I think the cheapest, fastest solution for now is the sandbag!||||reg||6
Z||000009||Spyder||01-31-2000||08:36 PM||||I would suggest moving the battery to the front. If you don’t have lowered springs or spindles on the front that might help and the sway bars all the way around.  Is it bouncy like a springy type or just bumpy?  Because on rough roads the ride will be bumpier than other cars (ever seen a race car hit a bump). You will not lose power to the circuitry because the circuitry uses the alternator which is in the rear.||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||01-31-2000||08:37 PM||||The starter is towards the front of the engine compartment and the car is not very long to begin with. If you use a real heavy gauge wire, there won't be any problems. You really ought to get that battery out of the trunk though. That's dead weight hanging behind the rear axles and it will probably affect cornering too.||||reg||1
Z||000011||WKDFIRO||01-31-2000||08:50 PM||||Don't forget that you are still using 84 springs in the front (at least you make no mention of changing any springs.) Before you load down the front get so springs from a GT. The bounce rates are significantly less! I had to do this when I changed to a 16 inch wheel. I had a 4 cyl and the springs were really mushy and not set up for anything but a comforatable ride. Stiffer springs, like Eibach would also take care of this problem. <P>If you are using stock springs you are going to have to preload the springs, the Konis can do only so much! Also, I have heard many times that the hardest settings on Konis are actually strong enough to break the shock mounts on 84-87 in brutal driving or an unseen pothole on the highway! In GTs that I have ridden in the Konis were set to softest for typical everyday driving. Good luck!||||reg||2
Z||000012||LT----1||02-01-2000||01:19 AM||||You know what? I am not sure what springs I have in front. I think they have been cut but I can't tell. The front end is somewhat lowered. Bought the car this way. What year GT springs do you recommend? It's so hard to find GT springs at the boneyard. I wonder if the fiero store sells them. You mentioned eibach springs. Which ones specifically would solve this problem? It might be worth a try this spring to see IF the GT springs will solve my bouncing problem. BTW, with the car sitting in the garage and me pushing down on the fender wells, everything is pretty stiff. The front end moves maybe 1/2 to 1" down and no rebounds.||||reg||6
Z||000013||LT----1||02-01-2000||01:21 AM||||And I forgot to mention the following:<P>1. 16" wheels<BR>2. V rated tires<BR>3. Front are 205 or 225...<BR>4. Sway bars front and rear<BR>5. All new front end components.||||reg||3
Z||000014||lowCG||02-01-2000||06:33 PM||||Take a look at how close the bump stop is to your lower control arm,maybe you're bouncing off of that?||||reg||1
